L383: # BACKGROUND AND RATIONALE
L384: Sepsis is a potentially life-threatening condition characterized by a deregulated body’s response to infection causing injury to its own tissues and organs. Sepsis is the primary cause of death from infection, especially if not recognized and treated timely. Untreated it can evolve within minutes/hours to septic shock1.
L385: For clinical operationalization sepsis can be defined as an increase in the SOFA score of 2 points or more, which is associated with an in-hospital mortality greater than 10%.
L386: The SOFA score is widely employed in daily monitoring of acute morbidity and is also an acceptable surrogate marker of efficacy in exploratory trials of novel therapeutic agents in sepsis2.
L387: Our patient population can present themselves in the ED at different stages of the disease course. We defined 2 categories: sepsis and septic shock, the latter is the sickest population having already a high SOFA score on presentation. Patients with septic shock can be identified with a clinical construct of sepsis with persisting hypotension requiring vasopressors to maintain MAP ≥ 65 mm Hg and having a serum lactate level > 2 mmol/L (18 mg/dl) despite adequate volume resuscitation. With these criteria, hospital mortality is in excess of 40%. This is reflected in the international Surviving Sepsis Campaign (SSC) Database with over 130 000 registered patients. This database reports an overall mortality rate (sepsis plus septic shock) of 34.8%3. In Belgium, in 2017 12 743 patients suffering from sepsis or septic shock presented to an ED (FOD Volksgezondheid, sepsis data as per ICD-10-CM codes).
L388: Current practice guidelines for management of sepsis and septic shock are based on the Surviving Sepsis Campaign (SSC) Guidelines (http://survivingsepsis.org). These international guidelines focus on early identification and treatment of sepsis by the introduction of the Hour-1 bundle. The Hour-1 bundle encourages clinicians to act as quickly as possible to obtain blood cultures, administer broad spectrum antibiotics, start appropriate fluid resuscitation, measure lactate, and begin vasopressors if clinically indicated. Ideally these interventions would all begin in the first hour from sepsis recognition but may not necessarily be completed in the first hour4.
L389: The administration of fluids during the initial hemodynamic resuscitation remains a major therapeutic challenge. An initial fluid bolus (usually 500 ml-1000 ml) for hypotension or lactate ≥ 4 mmol/l is considered appropriate. Balanced crystalloids are considered the fluid of choice. Albumin derivates can be administered in addition to crystalloids in refractory shock. Starches should be avoided. Fluid resuscitation should be guided by the patients’ fluid responsiveness5.  Fluid responsiveness can be assessed through the administration of a fluid challenge, passive leg raise testing or pulse pressure variation and stroke volume variation6. Vasopressors are applied if hypotension persists during or after fluid resuscitation to maintain a mean arterial pressure of ≥ 65 mm Hg. If hemodynamic stability cannot be achieved with these measures, IV hydrocortisone at a dose of 200 mg per day can be considered4. Up to today, administration of a combination of Hydrocortisone, Ascorbic acid and Thiamine (HAT) is not current practice in Belgium. None of the centers participating in this study are using Thiamine or Vitamin C as adjunct for standard sepsis management.
L390: Sepsis is associated with an acute deficiency of Vitamin C and studies have reported that high doses of IV Vitamin C reduce organ injury and improve survival7–10. Vitamin C, also known as Ascorbic Acid, is a vitamin found in various foods. After almost a century of in vitro experiments and animal based studies, there is an abundance of plausible data for the protective effects of Vitamin C11,12. Also its safety has been well established over the years13. However, despite the proof-of-concept of the benefit of administering Vitamin C in patients with sepsis or septic shock, Vitamin C administration is not yet current practice. This is mainly due to the large variety in study set-ups, primary and secondary outcomes.
L391: RCTs on IV Vitamin C in sepsis
L392: In 2014, Fowler et al. performed a single centre RCT involving the administration of two different doses of Vitamin C to patients with severe sepsis14. Vitamin C reduced the SOFA score in a seemingly dose-dependent fashion.
L393: In 2016, Zabet et al. performed a double blinded single centre RCT involving the administration of IV Vitamin C to patients with vasopressor-dependent septic shock (25 mg/kg intravenous q6hr)15. Vitamin C caused a significant reduction in the duration of vasopressor infusion and an improvement in 28-day mortality.
L394: The CITRIS-ALI study (2019) is the first multi-centre RCT investigating high dose IV vitamin C (50 mg/kg intravenous q6hr) in patients with sepsis-induced Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)16. Vitamin C was initiated relatively late in the disease course (up to 48 hours after the development of end-organ failure meeting ARDS criteria). There was no difference at all in any of the primary endpoints (change in SOFA score, C-reactive protein (CRP) levels, or thrombomodulin levels). Patients in the Vitamin C group however had a lower mortality. Patients in the Vitamin C group also experienced more ICU-free days and more hospital-free days. It is important to point out the possibility that mortality differences between the control and intervention group could have biased the primary endpoint (SOFA score), due to selective elimination of sicker patients from the control group.
L395: Non-randomized trials on HAT
L396: Ascorbate and hydrocortisone synergize to improve endothelial function17. A before-after study by Paul Marik et al. involving the use of HAT in septic shock resulted in a dramatic decrease in mortality (8.5% in HAT group vs 40.4% in control group)18,19. Subsequently several centres have reported similar studies. However these studies are split, with some showing benefit and others finding no difference, depending on the study set-up and the endpoints investigated 20–23.
L397: RCTs on HAT
L398: In February 2020, the VITAMINS trial was published24. This is a multi-centre RCT of patients in septic shock which compares the administration of hydrocortisone alone versus HAT therapy. In total 216 patients were included. No impact was seen on vasopressor-free time (the primary endpoint) or mortality. HAT did cause an improvement in the SOFA score (the much-debated primary endpoint of CITRIS-ALI). However, since this is an isolated secondary endpoint, it cannot carry much weight.
L399: The main limitation of the VITAMINS trial lays in the fact that it is not a study of early metabolic resuscitation: the median delay from ICU admission to randomisation was 12 hours. Time delays prior to ICU arrival were not accounted for (which for some patients included inter-hospital transfer). A retrospective study by Long et al. suggests however that an early start of HAT therapy is important (ideally within < 6 hours)25.
L400: The last published study is the HYVCTTSSS trial26. This is an 80 person single-blinded RCT in patients with sepsis or septic shock comparing hydrocortisone (50 mg every 6 h for 7 days), vitamin C (1.5 g every 6 h for 4 days), and thiamine (200 mg every 12 h for 4 days) vs placebo (normal saline). There was no difference in the primary outcome of 28-day mortality. SOFA score at 72 hours was lower in the cocktail group. Interesting, and hypothesis-generating, in the group with a sepsis diagnosis within 48 hours of onset, mortality was lower. Severe hypernatremia (>160 meq/L) was more common in the treatment group than in the control group, 13 vs. 3 (p=0.005), leading to early termination of the study. Hydrocortisone is known to promote hypernatremia, but this is a very mild effect which should be easily managed with usual high-quality ICU care. This suggests that the hypernatremia observed in this study is more a reflection of the quality of the ICU care overall than of any specific intervention.
L401: Although it is generally accepted that in sepsis timing of therapies matters, much like stroke and AMI, the majority of these studies started quite late (up to 12 to 48 hrs post ICU admission) in the disease course with the administration of Vitamin C. As Vitamin C potentially prevents the development of multi-organ dysfunction by treating microvascular dysfunction, mitochondrial injury and oxidative stress, this late administration might miss its intended effect 27–29.
L402: For this reason, the emphasis of our study will be put on the early administration of Vitamin C, within 6 hours after arrival in the ED. We opted for a high dose IV Vitamin C only adjuvant therapy, in order to investigate the benefit of mono therapy. Hydrocortisone and thiamine are certainly indicated in certain patient groups but whether HAT therapy induces additional benefit remains uncertain. With the C-EASIE trial, the investigators plan to conduct a prospective, multi-centre, randomized, double blinded trial in patients presenting at the ED with sepsis or septic shock. We will investigate the potential benefit of early administration of high doses of Vitamin C (1.5 g bolus, 4 times a day during 4 days, started within 6 hours after admission) in addition to standard care in patients with sepsis or septic shock. By setting up this pragmatic comparative effectiveness trial, we hope to see a more rapid solution of shock and less deterioration from sepsis to septic shock, hereby reducing morbidity and mortality as well as the length of hospital stay in this patient population. Since Vitamin C is very cheap and safe, this simple intervention has the potential to not only be cost-effective but also cost-saving.
L403: # Assessment and management of risk
L404: This trial is categorised as an intermediate risk intervention clinical trial. Justification is provided in Appendix 1.
L405: # OBJECTIVES AND ENDPOINTS / OUTCOME MEASURES
L406: ## Primary objective
L407: The overall objective of the study is to evaluate the clinical efficacy of early administration of Vitamin C in addition to the standard of care in patients hospitalized with sepsis or septic shock.
L408: We will include patients with suspected infection and NEWS ≥ 5 (cfr 6.1 Inclusion criteria).
L409: These patients will be randomized into 2 study groups. The intervention group will receive intermittent infusion of Vitamin C (1.5 g every 6 hours, diluted in normal saline) during 4 days or until hospital discharge and is started within 6 hours after presentation (time of triage) in the ED. The comparator group will receive placebo (an intermittent infusion of saline).
L410: The primary objective is to determine the efficacy of early administration of Vitamin C by determining the average post-baseline patient SOFA score (on day 2-5 after patient inclusion) as compared to placebo.
L411: We aim to proof that through early admission of Vitamin C, patients who present at an early stage of the disease course will get less sick, resulting in a lower maximum SOFA score. However, administration of Vitamin C in our sickest population might not necessarily reflect in a lower maximum SOFA score, but rather in faster recovery (higher delta SOFA between 2 points in time). To capture both phenomena, we opted to look at the average post-baseline patient SOFA score as primary outcome supported by a maximum SOFA score as secondary outcome (more info about statistical analysis plan in 10.3).
L412: Each patient included in this study, both in the intervention and comparator group, will receive 4 scheduled visits post randomization on the 4 consecutive days following admission (see 7.8 Table of Procedures for more details). These visits are conducted by a trained study nurse or sub-investigator.
L413: ## Secondary objectives
L414: Secondary objectives are to evaluate other patient oriented outcome measures and cost-effectiveness of early administration of Vitamin C as compared to placebo.
L415: #### Clinical Severity
L416: - Maximal SOFA score
L417: #### Hospitalization
L418: - Length of hospital stay (days)
L419: - Length of ICU stay (days)
L420: #### Mortality
L421: - 28-day mortality
L422: #### Mechanical Ventilation
L423: - Ventilator days during hospital admission
L424: #### Steroid use
L425: - Total dose of steroids given (d1-d5)
L426: #### Vasopressor requirement
L427: - Total duration and dosage during hospital admission
L428: #### Renal replacement therapy (RRT)
L429: - RRT duration and need during hospital admission
L430: #### Health related quality of life
L431: - EQ-5D-5L health questionnaire at baseline, day 5, day 28 and month 3 in the view of a possible health economic analysis. If the patient’s clinical status doesn’t allow self-completion, the questionnaire may be completed by proxy.
L432: - Time to return to work (if applicable)
L433: #### Evaluate the safety of the intervention as compared to the control arm as assessed by:
L434: - Changes in creatinine, CRP and sodium over time (d1-5) (part of standard of care).
L435: - Changes in procalcitonin between d1 and d4.
L436: ## Endpoints
L437: Up to now, no core domains for clinical research in sepsis or septic shock are identified. Looking at studies about outcome measures of critically ill patients receiving ECMO or with acute respiratory failure, we can conclude that most important domains are life impact (physical, mental and cognitive health), death and adverse events30,31. Since both patient groups cover ICU survivors and the results are comparable, it is safe to say that these outcome sets also can be applied to our patient group.
L438: Both physical and mental health outcome parameters are included in our endpoints.
L439: ## Primary endpoint
L440: #### Clinical Severity
L441: SOFA score:
L442: - Average post-baseline patient SOFA score (on day 2-5 after patient randomization).
L443: - To avoid missing data for deceased patients, the maximum SOFA score of 24 will be assigned to mortality. Missing values for the non-deceased patients will be handled using likelihood-based methods and imputed when evaluating the maximal SOFA score (see statistical analysis plan).
L444: - The SOFA score is a morbidity/mortality prediction score based on the degree of dysfunction of 6 organ systems: the respiratory, cardiovascular, hepatic, coagulation, renal and neurological systems. Organ scores range from 0 to 4, with the best score being 0 and the worst score being 4. The higher the SOFA score, the higher the likely mortality.
L445: ## Secondary endpoints
L446: - 28-day mortality
L447: - Maximum SOFA score
L448: - Length of hospital stay (days)
L449: - Length of ICU stay (days)
L450: - Vasopressor requirement: total duration and dosage
L451: - RRT duration and need
L452: - Ventilator days
L453: - Total dose of steroids given (d1-d5)
L454: - Quality of life (EQ-5D-5L)
L455: - Time to return to work (if applicable)
L456: - Lab values :
L457: - Creatinine, CRP and Sodium on d1-5
L458: - Procalcitonin on d1 and d4
L459: ## Exploratory Endpoints
L460: Feasibility outcomes
L461: - Time from meeting eligibility criteria to the first dose of the study drug
L462: - Average monthly recruitment rate
L463: - Number of patients screened
L464: - Randomized to the screened patient ratio
L465: - Reasons for exclusion
L466: - Compliance with drug administration protocol
L467: # TRIAL DESIGN
L468: This study is a double-blinded, randomized, controlled clinical trial to evaluate the clinical efficacy (superiority) of early administration of Vitamin C in addition to the standard of care in hospitalized patients with sepsis or septic shock.
L469: The study will compare standard of care + placebo vs. standard of care with the investigational therapeutic agent, Vitamin C or Ascorbic Acid.
L470: The clinical outcomes of this study have been chosen based on core clinical outcome measures in critically ill patients and clinical outcome measures used in previous studies using Vitamin C.
L471: # STUDY SETTING
L472: The study is a phase 3b multi-centre trial.
L473: The study will be conducted in 8 hospitals throughout Belgium (Universitair Ziekenhuis Leuven, Universitair Ziekenhuis Antwerpen, Universitair Ziekenhuis Brussel, Algemeen Ziekenhuis Turnhout, Centre Hospitalier Universitaire de Liège, Université Libre de Bruxelles Hôpital Erasme, Centre Hospitalier Universitaire St-Pierre Bruxelles and GasthuisZusters Antwerpen). Patients will be recruited at the EDs of the participating sites.
L474: # ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA
L475: Sepsis remains a clinical and presumptive diagnosis: in all sepsis definitions, one has to have "suspected infection" as the cause of the current illness (Sepsis3). Therefore, selection of septic patients is still a subjective pitfall in many trials since no single objective inclusion criterion can be used. It is not our purpose to redefine the diagnosis of sepsis but our goal is to include these patients with suspected infection who are at risk for clinical deterioration and progressive organ dysfunction. Early detection of this population is important to timely start treatment, so a sensitive screening tool identifying all patients with higher risk for severe illness caused by an infectious disease is necessary.
L476: Overlooking all available literature and defining our selection criteria of acute illness in septic patients with high risk for deterioration, we selected the NEWS scoring system as our screening tool in this trial (cfr. Appendix 5).
L477: ## Inclusion criteria
L478: Participants eligible for inclusion in this trial must meet all of the following criteria:
L479: - Patient is ≥ 18 years old.
L480: - Patient has a ‘suspected infection’: this requires the combination of AB administration and body fluid cultures within the first 6 hours after ED presentation.
L481: - Patient has a NEWS score ≥ 5.
L482: NEWS is based on a simple aggregate scoring system in which a score is allocated to physiological measurements, already recorded in routine practice, when patients present to the hospital. Six simple physiological parameters form the basis of the scoring system: respiration rate, oxygen saturation, systolic blood pressure, pulse rate, level of consciousness or new confusion and temperature.
L483: ## Exclusion criteria
L484: Participants eligible for this trial must not meet any of the following criteria:
L485: - Patient (≥18 years old) or legally authorized representative (LAR) didn’t provide informed consent. Delayed informed consent can be applied in cases where the patient is critically ill and no LAR is available. (see 7.2)
L486: - AB administration as a single dose or as a prophylactic treatment.
L487: - AB administered without an accompanying body fluid culture according to the timeframe (within 6 hours after ED presentation).
L488: - ‘Do no intubate’ or ‘comfort measures only’ status.
L489: - Failure to randomize within 6 hours after ED presentation.
L490: - Weight < 45 kg.
L491: - Pregnant or breastfeeding.
L492: - Known allergy for Vitamin C.
L493: - Known history of oxalate nephropathy or hyperoxaluria.
L494: - Known history of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.
L495: - Known history of chronic iron overload due to iron storage and other diseases.
L496: - The patient is already on IV steroids for a reason other than septic shock.
L497: - Proven active COVID-19 infection (positive swab and/or CT scan positive for COVID-19 within 14 days prior to or at ED presentation).
L498: - Participation in an interventional trial with an investigational medicinal product (IMP) or device
L499: # TRIAL PROCEDURES
L500: ## Recruitment
L501: ### Patient identification
L502: Patient identification will happen at the triage of the ED. Adult patients with suspected infection are flagged. The principal investigator or sub-investigator will be informed. An information leaflet and poster will be available at triage to remind triage nurses of the trial.
L503: In a next step informed consent will be obtained (7.2) by the PI or a sub-investigator who is adequately trained, and to whom this task has been delegated by the PI (cfr. Delegation log). Informed consent (patient, LAR or delayed) will be obtained prior to any study specific procedures being completed. The information of pre-screened patients who didn’t give informed consent won’t be collected.
L504: In a next step, eligibility is checked. The resources needed for this eligibility check are access to medical history, current clinical presentation and vital signs. The NEWS parameters are all vital signs standardly taken at triage.
L505: Eligibility will be logged in the screening log. This will be in a strictly anonymous manner, and in no way (nor through the sponsor, nor through the investigator) this information can be traced back to the patient. This way we will generate an anonymous, aggregate data set.
L506: Next, the patient will be logged in the subject identification log and receive a study specific subject number.
L507: ### Screening
L508: Screening won’t require study specific procedures in addition to standard of care, with exception of a urine pregnancy test being performed in women of childbearing potential for whom a pregnancy test within 6 hours of ED presentation is not done as standard of care.
L509: ## Consent
L510: The aim is to conduct the trial on the basis of prior informed consent by the trial participants and/or their LAR. However, since some of these patients are critically ill and unable to give informed consent, a process of delayed consent, enrolling patients into the clinical trial and obtaining consent as soon as practical from either the patient or their LAR will be applied.
L511: Patient screening, eligibility check, randomisation and study drug administration needs to happen within 6 hours after ED presentation. The main goal is to obtain informed consent from the patient or LAR prior to randomisation. However, it is perfectly reasonable that we won’t be able to access the LAR in time to enrol the patient into the clinical trial, justifying the need for delayed consent.
L512: There is an abundance of evidence that Vitamin C is safe to administer.
L513: The process for obtaining and documenting initial and continued informed consent from potential trial participants will be conducted in accordance with ICH-GCP E6(R2), applicable regulatory requirements and internal SOPs.
L514: Participant Information Sheets will be provided in Dutch, French and English and in a format easily understood by the participant population. They will be approved by the EC and be in compliance with GCP, local regulatory requirements and legal requirements.
L515: Potential participants or their LAR will have the possibility to ask questions.
L516: All originally signed obtained Informed Consent Forms (ICFs) must be retained/archived in the Investigator Site File (ISF) at the Participating Site and must not be destroyed (even when a scanned copy is available) before expiration of the legal archiving term as defined in the protocol section entitled “Archiving”.
L517: Participants may voluntarily withdraw consent to participate in the trial for any reason at any time. The participant’s request to withdraw from the trial must always be respected without prejudice or consequence for further treatment. Consent withdrawal will be documented in the participant’s medical record. The PI must take into account the consequences of such withdrawal: (1) further use of personal data/Trial data, (2) use of human biological materials already collected, (3) safe transition to alternative treatment options, etc. as applicable.
L518: Trial data and samples collected before withdrawal can be used in the trial.  No new trial data or samples will be collected after withdrawal of the participant.
L519: ## Trial randomisation
L520: Patients in the trial will be randomly assigned to the intervention group or the control group.
L521: Ardena Gent NV, a Drug Product Development & Manufacturing firm, will blind, package, relabel the IMP kits and provide randomization. Site staff will be blinded to study arm allocation. Once inclusion and exclusion criteria are verified, participants will be randomly assigned a treatment kit through randomize.net.
L522: Randomization lists will be provided by Ardena Gent NV in a 1:1 allocation ratio stratified by site. Within each site, block randomization will be applied using varying block sizes. The online platform randomize.net will be used for randomization. It is web based and accessible 24/7. Each study site staff member able to include patients in the trial, will have a personal log in.
L523: ## Blinding
L524: Once the patient has been randomized, a patient treatment kit will be allocated. These pre-made kits will contain the blinded IV ampoules for the entire treatment period (16 doses - 4 days) for the patient.
L525: The treatment kit will contain 52 ampoules (48 to be administered, 4 reserve ampoules) of Vitamin C 500mg/5ml OR 52 ampoules of Normal Saline 5ml 9mg/ml. These ampoules have identical sizes and will be blinded by a cap and sticker so that they look identical. For administration, bedside nurses will have to dilute 3 ampoules in 50ml of Normal Saline. Vitamin C for IV use and normal saline are both colourless and odourless. It has been described that Vitamin C can turn yellowish, but only if stored for too long or under non ideal circumstances. This way, trial participants, as well as care providers will be blinded to intervention.
L526: Blinding, packaging, and distribution will be performed by Ardena Gent NV.
L527: The trial team opted for this strategy for several reasons:
L528: Timely administration of Vitamin C. Currently insufficient information about the stability of dissolved Vitamin C in Normal Saline is available. Since administration needs to happen within 6 hours after ED presentation, a fresh stock of prepared solutions should always be available on every site (and renewed every couple days). This would require extensive stability testing and a 24/7 on call pharmacist on every site to provide the following doses of Vitamin C.
L529: Logistics. Patients will be transferred during these 4 days from the ED to the ICU and potentially the ward. Having one pre-made patient kit with clear instructions will facilitate administration of the study drug.
L530: Final unblinding of all trial participants will happen after the creation of a locked analysis data set.
L531: ## Unblinding
L532: Breaking the blind will only take place where information about the participant’s trial treatment is clearly necessary for the appropriate medical management of the participant. In all cases the Investigator would be expected to evaluate the causality and expectedness of (S)AEs as though the participant was receiving the active medication.
L533: The following process will be installed:
L534: breaking the blind can only be done by the CI/PI/SubPI through the IRT system.
L535: if the person requiring the unblinding is not the CI/PI/SubPI, then that health care professional will contact the C-EASIE team (ceasie@uzleuven.be, 016 345820, 016340700 (only in case of emergency))  with an unblinding request for a trial subject and an assessment to unblind should be made in consultation with the clinical and research teams.
L536: on receipt of the treatment allocation details, the CI/PI or treating health care professional will continue to deal with the participant’s medical emergency as appropriate.
L537: the CI/PI documents the breaking of the code and the reasons for doing so on the eCRF, in the site file and medical notes. It will also be documented at the end of the study in any final study report and/or statistical report.
L538: the CI/Investigating team will notify the Sponsor in writing as soon as possible and preferably before unblinding happens.
L539: The written information (reason for unblinding) will be disseminated to the Data Safety Monitoring Committee for review in accordance with the data monitoring committee (DMC) Charter.
L540: As the investigator is responsible for the medical care of the individual trial subject (Declaration of Helsinki 3§ and ICH 4.3) the coding system in blinded trials should include a mechanism that permits rapid unblinding (ICH GCP 5.13.4). The investigator cannot be required to discuss unblinding if he or she feels that emergent unblinding is necessary.
L541: ## Baseline and follow up data
L542: Parameters should be obtained as part of routine clinical care. To avoid burden on clinical care in a time of a strained health care system, we will mainly collect lab values that are considered routine clinical care in sepsis. Only on day 1 and 4 a targeted blood sample will be collected for procalcitonin determination.
