at have been put in place to mitigate risks to participant safety:   Trial-specific adverse event reporting strategy: All SAE’s and severe AE’s will be collected for this trial which will be reviewed by an independent Data Monitoring Committee as defined by the protocol. Medically significant events as defined in the protocol will be captured on a Serious Adverse Event form.    Trial oversight committees (TSC and/or DMC), independent data review A Trial Steering Committee (TSC), Trial Management Group (TMG) and Data Monitoring Committee (DMC) are in place for this phase III trial. The DMC will meet after 50 patients have completed treatment and will review the study data and advise on the continuation of the trial. They will monitor all participating sites until all patients are recruited. The TSC will meet on average 3 times per year the first year and twice a year after that. The DMC will then meet annually to discuss the progress of the trial.    Clinical procedures Daily blood sampling during the day 1-5 are considered as standard care. Daily ABG may cause discomfort and pain to patient of regular sampling. Blood sampling will be performed by skilled staff.  Informed Consent The trial will involve critically ill patients who might be too ill or incapable to give their informed consent. A process of delayed consent, enrolling patients into the clinical trial and obtaining consent as soon as practical from either the patient or their LAR will be applied. |
L965: | Processes that have been simplified based on the risk adapted approach.   Harmonization of practices of all participating sites.  As this trial is multi-centric, including 8 different hospitals and multiple departments within each hospital, harmonization of practices will be challenging. We plan to tackle this by including a pre-trial training for each participating site. Furthermore, we plan regular TMG meetings as well as trial site monitoring.  Finally, we have foreseen a low patient inclusion rate to compensate for late Vitamin C administration (not within 6 hours) and patients admitted during the night or in the weekend (difficult dissemination of the study protocol, no study nurses available …).   Harmonization of multiple services involved. The patient will transfer from ED, through ICU, potentially to the ward during treatment period of the trial. This implies that many different people will be involved and need to be trained. Administration of medication will be simplified through the implementation of treatment kits that stay with the patient at all times and also include all the necessary trial information. Dedicated and trained study nurses will conduct daily patients visits and be able to inform and help staff of these different services.  Logistics of early administration. Since Vitamin C has to be administered within the 6 hours post ED presentation we evaluated every potential bottle neck during this process.  Patient identification will happen at ED triage. Patients with suspected infection and NEWS ≥ 5 are flagged. The NEWS parameters are all vital signs standardly taken at triage. Triage nurses will receive a pre-trial training.  Eligibility check. After an initial check, eligibility will be confirmed through REDCap. By logging on to the platform, the investigator can easily navigate through a checklist and perform a second eligibility check after which randomization will take place.    Delayed informed consent can be a solution in cases where the patient is critically ill and no LAR is available  And finally pre made treatment kits will be available at every site. They will be allocated to the patient once randomized.   Blinding of study medication. We opted to blind ampoules instead of premade sterile solutions to overcome storage and stability issues. Treatment kits will be available for every participating center and stored in ED. | Processes that have been simplified based on the risk adapted approach.   Harmonization of practices of all participating sites.  As this trial is multi-centric, including 8 different hospitals and multiple departments within each hospital, harmonization of practices will be challenging. We plan to tackle this by including a pre-trial training for each participating site. Furthermore, we plan regular TMG meetings as well as trial site monitoring.  Finally, we have foreseen a low patient inclusion rate to compensate for late Vitamin C administration (not within 6 hours) and patients admitted during the night or in the weekend (difficult dissemination of the study protocol, no study nurses available …).   Harmonization of multiple services involved. The patient will transfer from ED, through ICU, potentially to the ward during treatment period of the trial. This implies that many different people will be involved and need to be trained. Administration of medication will be simplified through the implementation of treatment kits that stay with the patient at all times and also include all the necessary trial information. Dedicated and trained study nurses will conduct daily patients visits and be able to inform and help staff of these different services.  Logistics of early administration. Since Vitamin C has to be administered within the 6 hours post ED presentation we evaluated every potential bottle neck during this process.  Patient identification will happen at ED triage. Patients with suspected infection and NEWS ≥ 5 are flagged. The NEWS parameters are all vital s