L988: Protocol amendments must be submitted to the Sponsor for approval prior to submission to the EC committee or FAMHP.
L989: # Rationale BEHIND INCLUSION CRITERIA
L990: In previous literature, many scoring systems were proposed to detect this population. Both SIRS and qSOFA are mentioned in the official surviving sepsis campaign guidelines, but recent studies conclude they have lower diagnostic accuracy, sensitivity and specificity than newer risk-stratification tools in predicting mortality or ICU transfer of critically ill patients (both in septic and non-septic populations). SIRS criteria are oversensitive and lack specificity in identifying sepsis, with almost half of ward patients developing signs of SIRS at some point during their hospital stay and the need for two or more SIRS criteria to define sepsis were not present in 12% of ICU-admitted septic patients. On the other hand, qSOFA is a retrospectively developed screening tool with mortality as primary outcome. This has not been compared by the authors with other well-known and equally performant risk-stratification tools and has not been prospectively validated upon our trial onset. Multiple trials comparing qSOFA with other scores showed it is more specific than SIRS but its criteria indicate actually existing decompensation rather than predicting future deterioration. The lack of sensitivity (54%) limits the applicability of qSOFA compared with physician judgment for the disposition of initially stable patients. A study of 30,677 patients with suspected infection in the ED or general ward area supports this hypothesis, finding that the National Early Warning Score (NEWS) was superior to qSOFA in predicting adverse outcomes, and that both systems were superior to the use of SIRS criteria. The NEWS is validated to identify acutely ill patients in the early stage of their illness (including sepsis) and is endorsed by different emergency health care societies. The NEWS was shown to be as good at discriminating risk of serious clinical deterioration and acute mortality as the best existing systems and better than most. (Smith, 2013)
L991: Overlooking all available literature and defining our selection criteria of acute illness in septic patients with high risk for deterioration, we selected the NEWS as our screening tool in this trial.
L992: NEWS is based on a simple aggregate scoring system in which a score is allocated to physiological measurements, already recorded in routine practice, when patients present to, or are being monitored in hospital. Six simple physiological parameters form the basis of the scoring system: respiration rate, oxygen saturation, systolic blood pressure, pulse rate, level of consciousness or new confusion and temperature.
L993: A score is allocated to each parameter as they are measured, with the magnitude of the score reflecting how extremely the parameter varies from the norm. The score is then aggregated. The score is uplifted by 2 points for people requiring supplemental oxygen to maintain their recommended oxygen saturation.
L994: Definition of new confusion: the patient has new-onset confusion, disorientation and/or agitation, where previously their mental state was normal – this may be subtle. The patient may respond to questions coherently, but there is some confusion, disorientation and/or agitation. This would score 3 or 4 on the GCS (rather than the normal 5 for verbal response), and scores 3 on the NEWS system.
L995: Defining suspected infection
L996: In accordance with the Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3), a suspected infection was defined as the combination of antibiotics and body fluid cultures within a specific time window (Seymour, 2016). If the antibiotic was given first, the culture sampling had to be obtained within 24 h. If the culture sampling was done first, the antibiotic had to be ordered within 72 h. The onset of infection was defined as the time at which the first of these two events occurred. We excluded all antibiotic doses administered as a single dose or as a prophylactic treatment. We excluded antibiotics administered without an accompanying body fluid culture according to the timeframe. We included fluid cultures form any clinical human body site or indwelling device. Culture types included bacterial, fungal, viral and parasitic.
L997: We will include patients with suspected infection and elevated NEWS ≥ 5.
L998: # SmPC
L999: Name of the medicinal product
L1000: VITAMIN C PANPHARMA 100 mg/ml solution for injection Active ingredient: Ascorbic Acid
L1001: QUALITATIVE AND QUANTITATIVE COMPOSITION
L1002: 1 ml solution for injection contains 100 mg Ascorbic Acid.
L1003: Other excipients: For the full list of excipients, see section 6.1.
L1004: PHARMACEUTICAL FORM
L1005: Solution for injection
L1006: CLINICAL PARTICULARS
L1007: 4.1 Therapeutic indications
L1008: Methemoglobinemia in childhood.
L1009: Vitamin C substitute in parenteral nutrition.
L1010: 4.2 Posology and method of administration
L1011: Posology:
L1012: Vitamin C substitute in parenteral nutrition:
L1013: Prophylaxis of vitamin C deficiency in long-term parenteral nutrition:
L1014: Adults 100 - 500 mg Ascorbic Acid i.v. per day (equivalent to 1 - 5 ml VITAMIN C PANPHARMA per day).
L1015: Children 5 - 7 mg Ascorbic Acid i.v./kg body weight per day.
L1016: Methemoglobinemia in childhood:
L1017: A single time dose of 500 - 1000 mg ascorbic acid (equivalent to 1 - 2 ampoules VITAMIN C PANPHARMA) is injected intravenously. Administration of this dose can be repeated, if required. The maximum dose of 100 mg/kg body weight/day should not be exceeded.
L1018: Posology and method of administration
L1019: The dose is administered slowly intravenously. Duration of administration is subject to the clinical picture and the lab parameters.
L1020: 4.3 Contraindications
L1021: VITAMIN C PANPHARMA must not be administered in case of hypersensitivity to Ascorbic Acid.
L1022: VITAMIN C PANPHARMA can only be administered after consulting a doctor.
L1023: - in oxalate urolithiasis
L1024: - in iron storage diseases (thalassaemia, haemochromatosis, sideroblastic anaemia).
L1025: 4.4 Special warnings and precautions for use
L1026: Intravenous injections in high doses of VITAMIN C PANPHARMA can result in the formation of calcium oxalate crystals and in acute renal failure due to kidney stones. This has been observed at doses as low as 1,5 – 2,5 g. Patients with susceptibility to renal calculi are recommended not to exceed a daily vitamin C dose of 100 to 200 mg. Patients with extreme or terminal renal insufficiency (dialysis patients) should not be given more than 50 -100 mg per day.
L1027: In patients with erythrocyte glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency who were administered high vitamin C doses (4 g per day) partly serious haemolyses have been observed in single cases. Therefore, exceeding the given dosing recommendations must be avoided.
L1028: One ampoule contains 64,4 mg sodium. This needs to be taken in consideration for patients under sodium controlled diet.
L1029: 4.5 Interaction with other medicinal products and other forms of interaction
L1030: Ascorbic Acid has a strong redox potential. Therefore, the chemical compatibility for concomitant administration with other medicinal products must be checked for each individual case.
L1031: 4.6 Pregnancy and lactation
L1032: It is not advisable to exceed the given dosage during pregnancy and lactation. Ascorbic acid is secreted into breast milk and crosses the placental barrier.
L1033: 4.7 Effects on ability to drive and use machines
L1034: Not known.
L1035: 4.8 Undesirable effects
L1036: The following scale of frequency has been established in the evaluation of undesirable effects:
L1037: Very common (≥ 10%)
L1038: Common (≥ 1% - < 10%)
L1039: Uncommon (≥ 0.1% - <1%)
L1040: Rare (≥ 0.01% - <0.1%)
L1041: Very rare (< 0.01% or unknown)
L1042: Respiratory and skin hypersensitivity reactions were reported very rarely.
L1043: Reporting of suspected adverse reactions
L1044: Reporting suspected adverse reactions after authorization of the medicinal product is important. It allows continued monitoring of the benefit/risk balance of the medicinal product. Healthcare professionals are asked to report any suspected adverse reactions to Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, Abt. Pharmakovigilanz, Kurt-Georg-Kiesinger-Allee 3, D-53175 Bonn,
L1045: Website: www.bfarm.de.
L1046: 4.9 Overdose
L1047: See section "Special warnings and precautions for use” regarding the risk of renal calculi and haemolyses.
L1048: PHARMACOLOGICAL PROPERTIES
L1049: 5.1 Pharmacodynamic properties
L1050: Pharmacotherapeutic group: Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C)
L1051: ATC Code: A11GA01
L1052: Ascorbic Acid is an essential active substance for humans. Ascorbic Acid and dehydroascorbic acid constitute an important redox system of great physiological significance.
L1053: Vitamin C acts as a cofactor in numerous enzyme systems due to its redox potential (collagen formation, catecholamine synthesis, hydroxylation of steroids, tyrosine and exogenous substances, biosynthesis of carnitine, regeneration of tetrahydrofolic acid and alpha-amidation of peptides, e.g. ACTH and gastrin). Moreover, a deficiency of vitamin C affects immune defense reactions, particularly chemotaxis, complement activation and interferon production. The molecular biological functions of vitamin C have not yet been fully explained.
L1054: Ascorbic acid improves the resorption of iron salts by reducing ferric ions and by forming iron chelates. It blocks the chain reactions in aqueous body compartments triggered by oxygen radicals. The antioxidative functions produce biochemical interactions in close relation to those of vitamin E, vitamin A and carotenoids. As yet it has not been proven entirely that ascorbic acid causes a reduction of potentially carcinogenic substances in the gastrointestinal tract.
L1055: 5.2 Pharmacokinetic properties
L1056: Ascorbic acid is absorbed in the proximal small intestine in a dose-dependent manner. Bioavailability drops with increasing dosage to 60 - 75% after 1 g, to approx. 40% after 3 g and approx. 16% after 12 g. The portion which is not absorbed is broken down by the large intestinal flora into CO2 and organic acids.
L1057: The maximal metabolic turnover of 40 to 50 mg/day in healthy adults is reached at plasma concentrations of 0.8 to 1.0 mg/dl. The total daily turnover is about 1 mg/kg body weight. Brief plasma concentrations of up to 4.2 mg/dl are achieved about three hours after applying extremely high oral doses.
L1058: Under these circumstances ascorbic acid is eliminated unchanged in the urine by up to 80%. The half-life is 2.9 hours on average. Renal elimination ensues via glomerular filtration and subsequent reabsorption in the proximal tubule. The upper limits for healthy adults are 1.34 ± 0.21 mg ascorbic acid/dl plasma in men and 1.46 ± 0.22 mg in women, respectively.
L1059: The total body content of ascorbic acid is at least 1.5 g following a high dose of about 180 mg daily.
L1060: Ascorbic acid is concentrated in the pituitary gland, adrenal glands, lenses of the eye and white blood cells.
L1061: 5.3 Preclinical safety data
L1062: Acute toxicity
L1063: See section 4.9 (Overdose)
L1064: Chronic/subchronic toxicity
L1065: Subchronic and chronic tests in rats did not show any evidence of substance-related effects.
L1066: Mutagenic and tumorigenic potential
L1067: Long-term studies in mice did not come up with any evidence of tumorigenic potential.
L1068: Cell culture and animal tests did not show any evidence of a mutagenic effect within the therapeutic dosage range.
L1069: Reproductive toxicity
L1070: Tests in two animal species with daily doses of up to 1000 mg/kg body weight did not show any foetotoxic effects.
L1071: Ascorbic acid is secreted into breast milk and crosses the placental barrier by means of simple diffusion.
L1072: 5.4 Other information
L1073: After administering doses in the gram range, the ascorbic acid concentration in the urine can be elevated to such a degree that the measurement of various clinical-chemical parameters (glucose, uric acid, creatinine, inorganic phosphate) is impaired. Likewise, gram doses can lead to false-negative results in the attempted detection of occult blood in the stools.
L1074: In general, chemical detection methods which are based on color reactions can be affected.
L1075: Vitamin sources and meeting vitamin needs
L1076: The required Vitamin C nutrient density can only be found in vegetables and fruits. Due to its enhanced oxidizability and solubility there are considerable vitamin losses during cooking (green vegetables up to 60%, potatoes up to 25%). Ascorbic acid in fruits is relatively stable due to the acid pH range. In leafy vegetables there is an enzymatic degradation by peroxidase starting immediately after the harvest (at room temperature more than 50% within 48 hours) that can be prevented by blanching, e.g. when preserving vegetables. The vitamin C content of cow's milk does not protect nursed infants from a vitamin C deficiency disease.
L1077: Deficiency symptoms
L1078: The clinical manifest vitamin C deficiency syndrome (scurvy or Moeller Barlow disease) develops slowly in the course of several months from the latent vitamin C deficiency condition. General diseases such as measles, epidemic hepatitis, severe traumas, malabsorption syndrome or long-term administration of various medicinal drugs (such as salicylate and tetracycline) can accelerate this process considerably.
L1079: Unspecific early symptoms are reduced physical fitness and objective psychometric functional disorders such as increased exhaustion or irritability. Later on there is increased capillary fragility with petechiae, microscopic hematuria, decreased infection resistance (mainly caused by reduced phagocytosis), accompanied by gingivitis (after tooth eruption), and finally extensive mucous membrane and skin bleeding (ecchymosis and suggillation), that first appear at the mucous membrane on the underside of the tongue and the gingival margin. A frequent accompanying syndrome is a hypochrome microcytic, often iron-refractory anemia.
L1080: At the same time, wound-healing is retarded and connective tissue scarring is impeded. In nursed infants there are also extremely painful subperiosteal haematoma and haemorrhages in the growth zones of the long tubular bones (M. Moeller-Barlow). Vitamin C deficiency can be fatal in adults as well as in nursed infants. Adequate treatment with ascorbic acid usually results in full recovery without long-term effects even in severe cases.
L1081: The etiology of the changes needs to be analyzed objectively by providing evidence of a greatly reduced ascorbic acid concentration in the blood plasma (less than 0.1 mg/dl or 6 μmol/l respectively) and in the white blood cells (less than 5 mg/dl or 280 μmol/l respectively). The vitamin C concentration in the blood plasma is an indicator how the vitamin C requirement is met.
L1082: Only in rare cases the minimum vitamin requirement (0.5 mg/dl in men, 0.55 mg/dl in women) is not met in healthy individuals in Germany. This also applies to pregnant and breast-feeding women. During breast-feeding over several months, however, the ascorbic acid concentration in plasma and milk often decreases. The same applies to preserved breast milk.
L1083: The increased vitamin C requirement of heavy smokers is usually met in young and median age adults. Risk groups include elderly patients above 65 years of age (men more frequently than women), persons with high alcohol consumption, smokers, pregnant and breast-feeding women as well as individuals with unbalanced diet. Long-term administration of medicinal drugs (mainly salicylates, tetracyclines and corticosteroids) can deplete the Vitamin C reserves.
L1084: Acute infections, severe liver parenchymal diseases, severe traumas and hemodialysis may result in a marked decrease of the vitamin C level in blood plasma and leukocytes. Lower vitamin C concentrations in plasma and leukocytes can also be the result of chronic infections, severe malabsorption syndromes and in the terminal stages of a tumor progression.
L1085: PHARMACEUTICAL PARTICULARS
L1086: 6.1 List of excipients
L1087: Sodium hydrogen carbonate, water for injection
L1088: 6.2 Incompatibilities
L1089: Ascorbic acid has a great oxidation reduction potential. Therefore, the chemical compatibility for concomitant administration with other medicinal products must be checked for each individual case.
L1090: 6.3 Shelf life
L1091: Shelf life is 24 months. Do not use this medicinal product after shelf life has expired.
L1092: 6.4 Special precautions for storage
L1093: Store the ampoules in their outer packaging to protect the contents from light.
L1094: Do not store above 25°C.
L1095: 6.5 Nature and contents of container
L1096: Original pack with 10 ampoules of 5 ml solution for injection (N2)
L1097: Hospital pack-size with 100 (10 x 10) ampoules of 5 ml solution for injection.
L1098: 6.6 Special precautions for disposal
L1099: No specific requirements.
