L1112: # 13054_2025_5383_MOESM2_ESM
L1113: Standard Operating Procedure for medication handling
L1114: | S-number:  Title protocol:  Chief Investigator: |
L1115: | --- |
L1116: Flow of study medication
L1117: - Delivery of study medication by Ardena Gent NV to pharmacy
L1118: - Delivery of study medication by pharmacy to on-site temperature controlled room
L1119: - Storage in temperature controlled room
L1120: - Assignment of treatment kit to patient
L1121: - Administration of study medication
L1122: - Treatment kit sent back to study team
L1123: - Destruction of unused ampoules
L1124: - Delivery of study medication by Ardena Gent NV
L1125: Ardena Gent NV will deliver the treatment kits to the pharmacy of each site. The site provides the contact details, opening hours and address of the pharmacy. A first delivery is foreseen including 20 kits. It is the responsibility of the site to request a new delivery to the Sponsor when stock is running low (less than 5 kits). The Sponsor and site agree on a delivery date (approximately 5 days after first notification).
L1126: - Delivery of study medication by pharmacy to on-site temperature controlled room
L1127: Every delivery includes a temperature logger of Ardena (monitors the temperature during transport) and a shipment form. The study team/pharmacist that receives the study medication needs to complete the shipment form, sign and date it and send a digital copy back to Ardena. A few days later the study team will receive a signed copy from Ardena as well as the temperature log of the shipment. Provide  these documents to the study team for filing in the Investigator Site File to ensure drug accountability.
L1128: A few screenshots from the shipment form:
L1129: - Check whether the identification of the shipped study medication is correct (compare left column with packaging material).
L1130: - Check whether your site has received the correct treatment kit IDs and whether the total number of treatment kits is correct.
L1131: - Sign and date the form, and e-mail it to Ardena (Annelies.Paridaens@ardena.com).
L1132: - Storage in a temperature controlled room
L1133: Treatment kits needs to be stored at room temperature (between 15 and 25°C). Always store the medication ampoules in the white packaging box to protect the ampoules from light. Extra labels will be provided by the Sponsor team during the Site Initiation Visit. As per GCP guidelines it is necessary that the storage room has limited access and has room temperature monitoring at all times. How to monitor the temperature?
L1134: If the room has air conditioning, the technical service of the study site should be able to monitor the temperature of the site. This temperature log needs to be printed out  biweekly and filed in the Investigator Site File.
L1135: If the room is not air conditioned, a temperature logger should be installed in the room. If needed, the Sponsor will provide a logger. The temperature log needs to be printed out  biweekly and  filed in the Investigator Site File.
L1136: What to do in case of a temperature  excursion?
L1137: - Relocate the medication as soon as possible to a room with a lower temperature (for example the hospital pharmacy).
L1138: - Place the medication in quarantine. Do not include new patients!
L1139: - Check the degree and the duration of the temperature excursion on the temperature logger.
L1140: - Inform the Sponsor as soon as possible by e-mailing ceasie@uzleuven.be and file all communication in the Investigator Site File.
L1141: - Based on stability information, the Sponsor will decide whether the medication can still be used or needs to be destroyed. File this communication in the Investigator Site File.
L1142: - Assignment of treatment kit to patient
L1143: - Treatment kit
L1144: Treatment kits contain 52 ampoules of Vitamin C 500 mg/5 ml or 52 ampoules of Normal Saline 5 ml 9 mg/ml. The ampoules have identical sizes and are blinded by a cap and sticker for an identical look.
L1145: - Assignment of treatment kit
L1146: After the informed consent form is signed and the patient is screened (please complete screening log  see online training manual), the patient is randomized through www.randomize.net. Please do not forget to:
L1147: - Complete the subject identification log (see online training manual).
L1148: - Store the confirmation e-mail from www.randomize.net in the Investigator Site File.
L1149: - Write down the kit number and patient number in the electronic patient file.
L1150: - Administration of study medication
L1151: - Administration
L1152: Patients need to receive 1.5 g of IV Vitamin C or placebo every 6 hours during 4 days. Study treatment should be initiated within 6 hours after ED presentation. To ensure that no administration of study treatment is missed, it is recommended that the Investigator prescribes all 16 doses at once (if possible).
L1153: Right before each administration, 3 ampoules of study medication need to be dissolved in 50 ml of NaCl 0.9%. The infusion bag needs to run through in 15 minutes. The infusion bags (50 ml) need to be provided by the study site (foreseen in the study budget, should not be charged to the patient). The Sponsor recommends that all infusion bags are added to the treatment kit prior to transport to the ward or ICU. The treatment kit always stays with the patient, and therefore moves from ward to ward.
L1154: In total, if the patient is not discharged before day 5, 48 ampoules need to be administered. The treatment kit contains 4 spare ampoules. All UNUSED ampoules need to be stored in the treatment kit to ensure drug accountability. All USED ampoules need to be thrown away for safety and hygiene purposes. After administration of all 16 doses, or in the event study treatment is stopped earlier, the treatment kit needs to be send back to the study team.
L1155: Nurses who administer the study medication DO NOT need to be logged in the Delegation of Responsibilities Log!
L1156: - Administration instructions
L1157: To ensure correct study medication administration, the Sponsor provides a ‘treatment kit information leaflet’. It is the responsibility of the study team to add this document to the treatment kit so that bedside nurses and physicians on the ward are well informed of the study.
L1158: - Registration
L1159: For each administration, the bedside nurse needs to complete the Drug Accountability Log. The Drug Accountability Log needs to be sent back to the study team together with the treatment kit after the last administration. It is the responsibility of the study team to add this document to the treatment kit so that logging is performed correctly.
L1160: - Treatment kit sent back to study team
L1161: Used treatment kits and the accompanying Drug Accountability Logs need to be sent back to the study team. It is the responsibility of the study team to crosscheck the Drug Accountability Log, the number of unused vials and the medication registered in the electronic patient file. The medication balance section should then be completed by the study team. If the number of unused vials does not match the Drug Accountability Log and no logical explanation can be found (for example: broken ampoule reported by bedside nurse), this is considered as a protocol deviation and the Protocol Deviation Log needs to be completed.
L1162: - Destruction of unused ampoules
L1163: After the CTC trial monitor has visited the site and has checked the drug accountability, used treatment kits can be destroyed. Unused ampoules can be sent to your local pharmacy for destruction. Please file all documentation of destruction in the Investigator Site File. The study site receives budget from the Sponsor for maximum two destructions throughout the trial.
L1164: # 13054_2025_5383_MOESM3_ESM
L1165: Supplement 2: Statistical analysis primary outcome (cLDA model) and secondary outcomes
L1166: The constrained longitudinal data analysis model (cLDA) 1 is a linear model wherein both the baseline and post-baseline values (d2-d5) are modelled as dependent variables, as opposed to a (longitudinal) ANCOVA model in which the baseline value is included as a covariate. Although the baseline measure is included in the response vector in cLDA, the true baseline means are constrained to be the same for both treatment groups due to randomization, and this analysis provides an adjustment for the observed baseline difference in estimating the treatment effects. The effect of Vitamin C is allowed to be specific at each of the four post-baseline days, and the primary analysis refers to the average of the four intervention effects.
L1167: The choice of the covariance structure for the five measurements in the cLDA model was based on the Aikake criterion. Center was added as a fixed factor in the model. The cLDA analysis is valid under the missing at random assumption (MAR) and assumes that the probability that an observation is missing depends only on observed values of the individual, but not on the missing ones. Missing values can occur to discharge or dropout. Patients who died within the first 5 days received the maximum SOFA score of 24 starting at the day of death. An inverse hyperbolic sign transformation was applied to handle the (right-) skewed distribution of the SOFA scores. This transformation refers to a natural logarithm after adding a value (such that zero values for the SOFA score can be dealt with. Results were presented after back transformation to the original scale. As such, the estimate for the treatment effect refers to a ratio instead of a difference.
L1168: Two subgroup analyses were planned in the FAS, verifying if the effect of Vitamin C depended on baseline SOFA and on the baseline NEWS score. For the baseline SOFA score, subgroups were defined as SOFA <6 vs SOFA >=6. In the cLDA analysis on both subgroups combined, the interaction between subgroup and effect of vitamin C was tested. The baseline NEWS score was not dichotomised. Instead, the baseline NEWS score was evaluated as a continuous moderator by including the main effect of the baseline NEWS score and the interaction between the baseline NEWS score and the effect of vitamin C in the cLDA model, allowing non-linearity for the relation of the baseline NEWS score (using restricted cubic splines with 4 knots). A similar unplanned exploratory analysis was performed treating the baseline SOFA score as a continuous moderator and visualizing the change of the effect of vitamin C as function of the baseline SOFA.
L1169: A linear model, additionally correcting for center, was used to compare the maximal SOFA score between both groups. For patients who were discharged, the observed maximum SOFA was used. A multiple imputation approach (20 imputed datasets) was used to handle the presence of missing values not due to death or discharge (since the observed maximum can be an underestimation for these patients). Multivariate imputation was performed for the daily SOFA scores (d1-d5) using in each treatment arm separately the fully conditional specification (FCS) approach2 based on linear models with as regressors the SOFA scores on the other days. The imputation and the analysis model used the transformed SOFA scores.
L1170: Event rates for death were estimated using Kaplan- Meier methodology.  The comparison at 28 days was based on a Z-test after log-log transformation of the survival estimates.
L1171: To evaluate differences in length of hospital stay and length of ICU stay, death during hospital (ICU) stay was treated as a competing risk, using a stratified Gray’s test for the comparison of both groups.
L1172: A generalised linear mixed model (i.e. a logistic regression model with a random effect of patient) with group and time as fixed effects (and their interaction), and correcting for center as fixed effect, was used to compare the daily (d2-d5) predicted probability of mechanical ventilation.  An odds ratio was reported for the comparison of the ‘mean probability’ over d2-d5.  Mechanical ventilation was put on zero when a patient was discharged (from the day of discharge on). For a deceased patient, the information was considered missing from the day after death on. The same modelling approach was used for vasopressor requirement, need of steroids and renal replacement therapy. Note that the estimates from these random-effects models refer to the predicted probability and the predicted effect for the average patient.
L1173: The QOL scores were compared between both groups using a multivariate normal model for longitudinal measurements, with the Aikake criterion to select the covariance matrix. Center was added as a fixed effect in the model. Note that the model was fit on information from baseline, day 5 (or day of discharge if discharge before day 5), day 28 and month 3. For lost-to-FU and deceased patients, the score was put on missing. Comparisons referred to the three specific timepoints after baseline.
L1174: References:
L1175: - Liang, K. Y. and Zeger, S. (2000). Longitudinal data analysis of continuous and discrete responses for pre-post designs. The Indian Journal of Statistics, Series B 62, 134–148
L1176: - Van Buuren, S. (2007), “Multiple Imputation of Discrete and Continuous Data by Fully Conditional Specification,” Statistical Methods in Medical Research, 16, 219–242.
